    "id": "en-security-retention",
    "title": "Security and Data Retention",
    "route": "/en/docs/settings/security-retention",
    "category": "Settings",
    "status": "Published",
    "owner": "Product Operations",
    "updatedAt": "2026-11-03",
    "version": "1.4",
    "readingTime": 2,
    "summary": "Assign access by responsibility and protect shared workstations and exported data. Set retention according to project requirements and preserve records needed for investigations.",
    "tags": [
      "Security",
      "Data Retention",
      "Permissions",
      "English"
    ],
    "contentHtml": "<h2>Least-Privilege Access</h2><p>Grant only the features and cameras needed for the job. Restrict administrator access, user management, system settings, log export, and broad recording downloads to designated roles. Review whether those permissions are still needed.</p><h2>Accounts and Shared Workstations</h2><ol><li>Use accounts attributable to individual users. Avoid prolonged sharing of privileged accounts.</li><li>Do not save passwords on public, temporary, or shared devices.</li><li>Lock the workstation when stepping away. Sign out when finished.</li><li>Update roles and camera access, or deactivate access, when responsibilities change, staff leave, or collaboration ends.</li></ol><h2>Recording, Alarm, and Log Retention</h2><p>Set retention according to camera count, bitrate, capacity, investigation needs, and project policy. Recordings, alarm records, response notes, and logs may use different periods. Preserve material needed for critical-event investigations and audits until that work is complete.</p><p>Before reducing retention or a capacity limit, assess the impact on open investigations. Preserve required records through the project’s process, then apply and verify the change. Check each data type’s retention window separately.</p><h2>Exports and External Sharing</h2><p>Before exporting recordings, logs, or configuration, establish the purpose, minimum scope, recipient, and required approval. Where redaction, watermarking, encryption, or access expiration is required, use project-approved processing and transfer methods. Do not assume each tool is native to the platform.</p><p>Record the exporter, time, source event or device, file scope, and storage location. Inspect the file before sharing to verify that it contains only the information required for the task.</p><h2>Periodic Review</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Review Focus</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Accounts and Roles</th><td>Administrator, temporary, and inactive accounts; alignment between permissions and responsibilities.</td></tr><tr><th>Camera Resources</th><td>Alignment between role scope and the current area of responsibility.</td></tr><tr><th>Storage and Retention</th><td>Capacity risk, recording and log periods, and records needed for open investigations.</td></tr><tr><th>Activity</th><td>Unusual sign-ins, key configuration changes, and required export records.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Project Policy Takes Priority</h2><p>These are general operating principles. Customer policy, the project contract, and applicable requirements determine password rules, retention periods, approval procedures, and security classifications. Ask the project owner to resolve conflicting requirements before configuring the platform.</p>",
